        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 86,
        "headingId": 375,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Ovary</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:51:50"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62264,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 86,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>It arises in children and young women, as well as in some phenotypically female individuals with gonadal dysgenesis {10726633; 20097412}. It typically presents with abdominal pain or an abdominal mass and elevated serum LDH. Rarely, serum hCG may be elevated, particularly if syncytiotrophoblastic giant cells are present. Paraneoplastic hypercalcaemia may occur rarely. Most patients present with stage&nbsp;I disease. Bilateral involvement is grossly evident in about 10% {10726633; 20097412; 29331749}. The serum AFP level is not elevated in patients with pure dysgerminoma.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:51:50"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62265,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 86,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Dysgerminoma is the most common malignant germ cell tumour. It accounts for about 1% of all ovarian malignancies {15863154}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:51:50"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62266,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 86,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:51:50"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62267,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 86,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "<p>Chromosome&nbsp;12 abnormalities, typically isochromosome 12p or 12p amplification, are seen in 80% of dysgerminomas {21523721}. <em>KIT</em> mutation (exon&nbsp;17, codon&nbsp;816) is present in 30&ndash;50% of dysgerminomas {21523721; 22937135; 17274819} and <em>KIT</em> amplification in 30% {21523721}. In patients with gonadal dysgenesis, dysgerminoma may arise in association with gonadoblastoma (see <a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/chaptercontent/34/102\" target=\"_blank\">section </a><em>Gonadoblastoma</em>).</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-08-03T18:04:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62268,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 86,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Tumours are typically about 15&nbsp;cm and appear fleshy, yellow or cream-colored, solid, and lobulated. Cystic degeneration, haemorrhage, and necrosis may be present. Calcified areas may indicate a focus of gonadoblastoma in the tumour. About 20% are bilateral, although the contralateral tumour may not be evident on macroscopic examination.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:51:50"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62269,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 86,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "<p>Sheets and nests of monotonous tumour cells are separated by thin fibrous septa containing lymphocytes. Less common patterns include cords, trabeculae, solid tubules, and pseudoglands. Tumour cells are polygonal, with well-defined cell borders, abundant clear or eosinophilic cytoplasm, and one central nucleus with one or two prominent nucleoli. Nuclear contours may have an angular, squared-off appearance. Mitoses are common. Scattered syncytiotrophoblastic cells are present in a minority of tumours {7270783}. The surrounding stroma may contain poorly formed granulomas, which may obscure the tumour, especially in metastatic sites. Rarely, dysgerminoma may contain foci of spermatocytic tumour&ndash;like cells {28614211}. Dysgerminoma is immunohistochemically positive for SALL4 {19295406}, OCT4 {15371950}, LIN28 {22034885}, NANOG, KIT (CD117) {16056250}, and D2-40 {19483629}. Cytokeratins may be focally positive {16867864}. EMA, CD30, and glypican-3 (GPC3) are negative (see <a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/attachment/34/2/13160\" target=\"_blank\">#13160</a>Table <a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/attachment/34/2/13160\" target=\"_blank\">#13160</a>).</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-09T15:18:27"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62270,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 86,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:51:50"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 469,
